The RA changes with an increasing deformation level (5, 10, 15% and max) for IH430 and IH350 variants are presented in  Fig. 4 
                         . As the strain increases in the static tensile test and the martensitic transformation progresses, the amount of RA decreases. The graph of RA fraction as a function of strain ( Fig. 5 
                         ) shows that in the first stage of deformation, RA decreases faster in the case of sample IH450, which is directly related to the lower carbon content in RA and thus its lower stability. At the time of the fracture, however, the proportion of RA in variant produced using different parameters stabilizes at a ratio close to the initial one. This phenomenon, taking into account the invariability of other factors during deformation may result from the dependence of RA stabilization on a grain size.

The mechanical properties results are presented in  Table 3 
                         . Both variants of heat treatment resulted in relatively high yield strength of 423 and 461 MPa for IH450 and IH350, respectively. Uniform elongation is also at a reasonable level ∼21% for both variants. The uniform elongation close to the total elongation and a low value of YS 0.2 /UTS indicate the high strain hardening occurring in the range of uniform elongation. In the true stress – true strain curve (shown in  Fig. 7 
                         ), it can be observed that for the IH450 variant at a strain of about 0.02, a slight inflection point occurs in the curve, which may indicate the initiation of the martensitic transformation.
